'''''Verbena rigida''''', known as '''slender vervain'''<ref name = RHSPF/><ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |access-date=2014-10-17}}</ref> or '''tuberous vervain''', is a [[flowering plant|flowering]] [[herbaceous plant|herbaceous]] [[perennial plant]] in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] Verbenaceae. It is [[native plant|native]] to Brazil and Argentina, and is not fully [[hardiness (plants)|hardy]] in [[temperateness|temperate climates]], where consequently it is grown from seed as an [[annual plant|annual]].

Growing to {{convert|60|cm}}, it has a spreading habit, with stalkless toothed leaves and clusters of bright purple or magenta, scented flowers, held on branched stalks, in summer. Numerous [[cultivars]] have been selected for garden use.<ref>{{cite book|title=RHS A-Z encyclopedia of garden plants|year=2008|publisher=Dorling Kindersley|location=United Kingdom|isbn=978-1405332965|pages=1136}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rhs.org.uk/Gardens/Hyde-Hall/About-HydeHall/Plant-of-the-month/August/Verbena-rigida |title=Verbena rigida / RHS Gardening |access-date=August 2, 2012 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121127224435/http://www.rhs.org.uk/Gardens/Hyde-Hall/About-HydeHall/Plant-of-the-month/August/Verbena-rigida |archive-date=November 27, 2012 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://ag.arizona.edu/pima/gardening/aridplants/Verbena_rigida.html |title=Verbena rigida |website=ag.arizona.edu |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060622224508/http://ag.arizona.edu/pima/gardening/aridplants/Verbena_rigida.html |archive-date=2006-06-22}}</ref>

The species has gained the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref name = RHSPF>{{cite web | title = RHS Plant Selector - ''Verbena rigida'' | url = https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/60552/verbena-rigida/details | access-date = 17 February 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url = https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/pdfs/agm-lists/agm-ornamentals.pdf | title = AGM Plants - Ornamental | date = July 2017 | page = 106 | publisher = Royal Horticultural Society | access-date = 5 February 2019}}</ref>

